[test-suite name=process
  [doc [#
conformance fixtures for cx-stdlib/process (spec/std-lib/process.md §6).

   Each program imports the bundled module via [?lib 'cx-stdlib/process']
   (resolver MUST be quoted) then calls [$process:fn args]. The LAST
   expression is the rendered result.

   DETERMINISM / EFFECTFUL MODULE: cx-stdlib/process is impure Tier-B
   runtime (§1). EVERY public function is `impure` (§3) and operates on a
   spawned child process, so EVERY one requires the `subprocess`
   capability — both the spawn-family entry points AND the operations on
   an already-spawned child (§7: "Every function in this module operates
   on a spawned child process and therefore requires the `subprocess`
   capability"). The stdlib eval runner evaluates each case under
   `code.new_env()`, which grants NO capabilities (deny-by-default per
   spec/core/security.md §2). At the effect point the active set is
   checked FIRST; with the `subprocess` grant absent the function raises
   E_CAP_DENIED = `cx-err:CXER0271` (security.md §4: "a denied effect at
   the effect point raises CXER0271"; process.md §7: effectful functions
   "raise cx-err:CXER0271 when the grant is absent").

   Consequently the deterministic, runner-faithful, spec-grounded
   assertion for the happy-path call of every process function is the
   capability-denied error CXER0271 — real subprocess side effects
   (spawning binaries, signalling, allocating ptys) are neither
   reproducible nor safe inside the conformance harness, and AUTHORING.md
   ("Capability-gated / effectful") directs us to fixture the
   capability-denied error rather than real side effects. Each public
   function therefore gets a CXER0271 case exercising its real call shape
   (correct arity / arg kinds per the §3 [?def] signature). Several
   functions additionally get distinct call-shape cases for spec-called-out
   behaviors (argv-injection literal, empty-argv, env augment/clear, cwd,
   timeout, $check, pipeline stages, signal atoms) — all still gated to
   CXER0271 under the runner's empty capability set.

   PROCESS-SPECIFIC ERROR CODES (CXER4000–CXER4012, §5): these are
   documented behaviors that fire only AFTER the capability check passes
   (a granted spawn that then hits empty-argv, missing-cwd, not-found,
   timeout, pipefail+check, closed-handle, non-group-leader, pty-unsupported,
   encoding-invalid, etc.). Under the runner's empty capability set the
   capability check short-circuits FIRST, so a correct implementation
   raises CXER0271 — NOT the CXER4xxx code — for every one of these
   programs. Asserting CXER4001/4003/4006/4007/4009/4011/4012 here would
   therefore FAIL a correct implementation (a wrong fixture is worse than
   a missing one, AUTHORING.md "Grounding"). They are intentionally NOT
   asserted as out-err in this suite; their scenarios are documented in
   the spec's §6 conformance list and belong in a capability-granted
   harness (conformance/stdlib/process.cxd) once the host-grant fixture surface
   exists. See the report's `ambiguous` list.

   NOTE: no inter-fixture comment blocks appear between cases (a comment
   between sections risks capture into the preceding section payload);
   all explanatory prose lives in this doc header or in tags.
#]]

 [case id=process-001-run-cap-denied level=core
  [tags process run capability CXER0271]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/process']
[$process:run ("echo", "hello")]
#]]
  [out-err [#
cx-err:CXER0271
#]]
 ]

